answer:
**First, what needs protecting?** Builder collects fields, then `build()` creates an immutable object. If each thread uses its own builder instance (the normal case), there is nothing shared and no locking is needed. Thread-safety questions arise when (a) a single builder is shared across threads, or (b) the builder uses shared state such as an id generator, a cache of built objects, or a singleton builder.

**Make the product immutable.** Fields are `private final`, set in the constructor from the builder, with defensive copies of mutable inputs. An immutable result is safe to publish to any thread.

**Shared sequence number with atomics.** Say each built object gets a unique id:

```
class Order {
  private static final AtomicLong SEQ = new AtomicLong();
  private final long id; private final String item; private final int qty;
  private Order(Builder b){ id = SEQ.incrementAndGet(); item = b.item; qty = b.qty; }
  static class Builder {
    private String item; private int qty;
    Builder item(String i){ item = i; return this; }
    Builder qty(int q){ qty = q; return this; }
    Order build(){ return new Order(this); }
  }
}
```

`AtomicLong.incrementAndGet` uses compare-and-swap (CAS), so threads never block each other; they retry on contention.

**If one builder must be shared,** hold the setters' state in `AtomicReference` or snapshot-and-validate in `build()`. Note that several fields set together are not one atomic operation. For multi-field consistency, either keep an immutable snapshot object in an `AtomicReference` and swap it with CAS, or accept a lock. Be honest that atomics only help for single-variable invariants.

**Demo.** Start two threads (or an `ExecutorService`) each building 1000 objects, join them, and assert all ids are unique and the count is 2000.

**Trade-offs.** CAS avoids lock contention and deadlock but spins under heavy contention; `synchronized` is simpler and correct for compound invariants.

**Avoid:** claiming Builder is inherently unsafe, and `volatile` as a substitute for atomicity.
